WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:09.260
We recorded something very weird.

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:11.180
Something amazing.

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:14.660
Something that absolutely blew our minds.

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:18.730
And so we’re making a video that is different
for us.

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:24.230
And the fact that it happens over the course
of 20 minutes is absolutely astonishing, it

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:26.800
is a very brief amount of time.

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:33.690
But it also means that we have made the possibly
incorrect decision of sharing it with you

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:36.190
unedited and in real time.

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:39.730
It’s just so cool that we couldn’t bring
ourselves to edit it.

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:41.430
We didn’t want to speed it up.

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:48.649
We wanted it to take its course over the amount
of time that it took in the real world.

00:00:48.649 --> 00:00:54.039
But of course, we also don’t have enough
to say to fill up 20 minutes of video...so

00:00:54.039 --> 00:00:59.799
the narration will be coming in and out as
the...event...progresses.

00:00:59.799 --> 00:01:05.140
If you don’t have a full 20 minutes to watch
it, we have put the time codes of those narrations

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:06.280
in the description.

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:10.940
You can click on them and go to the moment
in the video where I will be talking about

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:12.600
what’s happening.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:16.820
So, to begin...Daphnia are a type of crustacean.

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:20.860
They’re known for the way they seem to jump
while they swim.

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:27.080
And one day, about a year ago—coming from
a cold pond in winter—a daphnia laid an

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:34.180
egg that eventually made its way under our
microscope, that large, dark oval you see

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:38.520
there taking up the upper left corner of the
screen, that is the Daphnia egg.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:44.220
But we’re not really going to talk about
Daphnia today because, as previously mentioned...things

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:46.020
got weird.

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:52.130
At the bottom corner there, you can see that
the egg is...leaking?

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:56.420
If you know nothing about the reproductive
habits of water fleas, and it’s totally

00:01:56.420 --> 00:02:01.530
understandable if you don’t, there’s a
sense of order to that movement that might

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:08.220
make it seem innocuous, a slow movement from
one oval to another as the leaking substance

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:10.660
becomes enveloped in a membrane.

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:13.510
It looks, at least, intentional.

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:19.470
But James, our master of microscopes, has
spent hours every day looking at the samples

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:20.470
he collects.

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:27.340
And when he saw this, he knew something very
strange was going on—he just didn’t know what.

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:31.170
So, he did the obvious thing and hit record.

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:33.420
So...watch with us…

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.220
Ok, is it done?

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:57.550
Did it do the thing?

00:03:57.550 --> 00:04:03.280
There is something vaguely intestinal about
what we’re watching here like the egg is

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:06.570
evacuating a part of it- Oh...no...there’s
more.

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:08.160
It’s still coming.

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:15.599
Ok, this is it.

00:04:15.599 --> 00:04:19.600
That is the last bit of whatever the goop
was.

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:27.400
It has transferred itself out of the egg and
into this...brown blobby thing.

00:06:58.460 --> 00:07:07.280
Ok, over the last few minutes, our misshapen
blob took on a more definite, spherical shape.

00:07:07.289 --> 00:07:15.560
Also, whatever came out of the daphnia egg
appears to be well and truly alive.

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:22.259
And as James changes focus, we can see that
this larger sphere appears to contain not

00:07:22.260 --> 00:07:30.280
just goop, but smaller spheres bound together
by that enclosing membrane.

00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:37.500
They’re giggling...they’re moving...they're
alive...they are cells.

00:08:21.380 --> 00:08:27.319
Now this started to seem like something we
might understand...this egg, it seems, was

00:08:27.319 --> 00:08:32.310
infected with something...and that something
had succeeded.

00:08:32.310 --> 00:08:37.800
It was an intruder that--having got what it
needed from its host--was moving on to the

00:08:37.800 --> 00:08:40.560
next phase of its life cycle.

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:45.740
And while the bulk of this image might consist
of the Daphnia egg, what we’re actually

00:08:45.740 --> 00:08:51.340
watching—the leaking, the cell-forming,
the swimming—is the reproduction of

00:08:51.340 --> 00:08:53.700
a parasite.

00:12:04.820 --> 00:12:09.700
It took months of searching, but we later
learned that this parasitic species is some

00:12:09.700 --> 00:12:17.910
type of oomycete, which, if you know your
science greek, you’ll recognize as a “egg fungus.”

00:12:17.910 --> 00:12:24.440
Now, it’s actually not a fungi, but it does
behave like a fungus in many ways.

00:12:24.440 --> 00:12:30.860
And oomycetes are responsible for some of
the most harmful plant pathogens, including

00:12:30.860 --> 00:12:34.690
the one responsible for much of the Irish
potato famine.

00:12:34.690 --> 00:12:40.380
They’re most closely related to algae, but
they aren’t really very closely related

00:12:40.380 --> 00:12:41.380
to anything.

00:12:41.380 --> 00:12:48.240
And the cells inside the membrane are zoospores,
they have flagella and the membrane is called

00:12:48.240 --> 00:12:49.460
the sporangium.

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:56.000
But the oomycete is still not done here, and
the strangest part is yet to come.

00:15:19.060 --> 00:15:26.740
In its own creeping way, the bundle here looks
like it’s expanding, as the zoospores inside

00:15:26.750 --> 00:15:32.790
swim more freely and energetically, they collide
against each other, taking up more and more

00:15:32.790 --> 00:15:36.490
space like a gas heating up.

00:15:36.490 --> 00:15:41.880
Eventually, we imagine, they’re gonna take
up so much space that the sporangium will

00:15:41.880 --> 00:15:47.000
rip apart, but not yet.

00:21:03.160 --> 00:21:10.679
Now, the pack of zoospores is getting bigger—their
movements look almost frantic, pushing and

00:21:10.679 --> 00:21:12.909
pushing against the sporangium.

00:21:12.909 --> 00:21:19.549
They are waving their flagella, triggered
by some stimulus, bouncing off of each other,

00:21:19.549 --> 00:21:21.070
straining the membrane.

00:21:21.070 --> 00:21:29.529
The Sporangium is getting larger and larger,
and then one zoospore breaks away….the membrane,

00:21:29.529 --> 00:21:36.659
though invisible, has now definitely broken,
and it peels back and all of its zoospore

00:21:36.659 --> 00:21:43.360
friends begin to follow...each of them searching
for a new host...each with the potential of

00:21:43.360 --> 00:21:49.179
starting this whole process all over again,
to the definite detriment of the next generation

00:21:49.180 --> 00:21:50.680
of daphnia.

00:21:50.680 --> 00:21:53.840
That was wild.

00:21:59.460 --> 00:22:04.700
Of course we wish we could have captured more
of the beginning of this process...but from

00:22:04.710 --> 00:22:10.360
the completion of the evacuation from the
egg to release of the sporangium was less

00:22:10.360 --> 00:22:13.450
than 20 minutes in real time.

00:22:13.450 --> 00:22:17.960
It’s hard to be on the side of these little
parasites, but that doesn’t mean that they

00:22:17.960 --> 00:22:20.320
aren’t fascinating!

00:22:20.320 --> 00:22:27.200
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:22:27.200 --> 00:22:30.809
Sometimes, apparently, we’re gonna do it
in slightly different ways.

00:22:30.809 --> 00:22:35.789
We’re gonna let what happens on the other
end of the microscope dictate what we do here

00:22:35.789 --> 00:22:39.159
on this channel, and we hope you are ok with
that.

00:22:39.159 --> 00:22:45.320
If you are, you are welcome to join the community
of people who helps us make these videos,

00:22:45.320 --> 00:22:49.059
including all of the names that are on this
screen right now.

00:22:49.059 --> 00:22:50.989
Thank you for joining our Patreon.

00:22:50.989 --> 00:22:56.299
If you want to see more from our Master of
Microscopes James, check out Jam and Germs

00:22:56.299 --> 00:22:57.470
on Instagram.

00:22:57.470 --> 00:23:01.659
And if you want to see more of what we’re
up to, there’s always a subscribe button

00:23:01.659 --> 00:23:03.070
somewhere nearby.

